// A grammar, expressed as data. `Rule` is a PEG combinator tree — sequence,
// ordered choice, repetition, a rule reference — and a `Production` pairs one
// rule with everything the passes downstream need to know about it: the CST node
// name, the label errors should blame, the scope a highlighter should paint, and
// the function that lowers a matched node to the caller's AST. One `Grammar` is
// then the whole description of a language, interpreted by the driver in
// `parse.aipl`, exactly as `lexer.aipl` interprets a `TokenRule[]`.
//
// This file is data and one pass over it (`link`); nothing here parses. The
// split matters because the same grammar value is meant to feed more than the
// parser — an EBNF dump, a highlighter, a formatter — and each of those wants
// the description without the driver.
//
// **A rule set is written against a token *kind* type `K`**, the same `K` a
// `lexer.aipl` rule set produces, so a language's lexer and grammar compose
// directly: `Term(Name(""))` matches any token whose kind is a `Name`, payload
// ignored (that's `same_case`, which is why `K` carries the `variant` bound).
//
// Two authoring notes that are compiler limits rather than design:
//
//   - An integer literal does not flex through a constructor, so `Many(r, 0)` is
//     a type error (the `0` types as `i64`, the payload is `u64`). Use the
//     `many`/`many1` helpers below, which absorb it once.
//   - Most `Rule` constructors mention no `K` (`Spelling`, `Named`, `Then` over
//     an array of them), so nothing in them pins the type parameter. Write each
//     production's rule as an *annotated* binding — `let r: Rule<Kind> = ..` —
//     and every constructor inside it resolves from that annotation. The `.test`
//     blocks here and in `parse.aipl` are written in that idiom throughout.
import { Span, equal as ==, map, not_equal as !=, try_map } from builtins;
import { Cst } from "./cst.aipl";

// Why a parse failed: a message, and the span of the offending source (absolute
// into the parsed string — slice it to show the offender, or hand it to
// `caret_block.aipl` for the rustc-style underline). Mirrors `lexer.aipl`'s
// `LexError`, and for the same reason: the two halves of a front end should
// report failure in one shape.
//
// A *grammar* error — an unresolved rule reference, a rule that can loop forever
// — has no source to point at and carries the empty `0..0`.
struct ParseError { message: str, span: Span }

// What a failed parse said — or, when it unexpectedly *succeeded*, a note
// carrying what it produced, so a broken assertion says which way it broke.
//
// For `.test` blocks, which is where the distinction matters: an assertion on a
// message that silently parses instead reads as a mismatched string with no clue
// why. Pairs with `map_ok`, which is what turns a parse of any AST type into the
// `str!ParseError` this takes: `parse_x(src).map_ok(render_x).failure_message()`.
pub fn failure_message(self: str!ParseError) -> str {
    match (self) {
        ok(s) => `unexpectedly parsed: {s}`,
        err(e) => e.message,
    }
}.test({
    let failed: str!ParseError = err(ParseError { message: "expected `)`", span: 3..4 });
    assert(failed.failure_message() == "expected `)`");

    let parsed: str!ParseError = ok("(a b)");
    assert(parsed.failure_message() == "unexpectedly parsed: (a b)");
})

// One precedence level of a `Climb`: the operators that share it, how tightly
// they bind (`power`, higher wins), and whether they associate to the right.
// Levels are matched in order, so a spelling belongs to exactly one.
struct Prec { power: u64, right: bool, ops: str[] }

// One rule shape, as plain data. Ordered choice makes this a PEG, not a CFG:
// `OneOf` commits to the first alternative that matches, so alternatives are
// tried in the order written and there is no ambiguity to resolve.
//
// - `Term(k)`: any one token whose kind shares `k`'s constructor — the payload
//   is ignored, so `Term(Name(""))` matches every identifier. The one place a
//   rule looks at a token's *kind* rather than its text.
// - `Spelling(s)`: one token whose source text is exactly `s`. Punctuation and
//   keywords, without having to name the kind that lexed them.
// - `Then(rs)`: every rule in order; the matched children concatenate.
// - `OneOf(rs)`: the first alternative that matches, in order.
// - `Many(r, min)`: `r` repeated until it stops matching, at least `min` times —
//   `0` is a PEG `*`, `1` a `+`. A rule that can match empty input would spin
//   here forever, so the driver refuses one outright rather than hanging.
// - `Maybe(r)`: `r`, or nothing. Always matches.
// - `Named(n)`: a reference to the production called `n`. `link` rewrites every
//   one of these to a `NamedIdx`, so the driver never compares strings.
// - `NamedIdx(i)`: a reference to production `i`, and the *only* rule that
//   builds a CST node — one `CTree(i, ..)` per reference matched.
// - `ListOf(item, sep, trailing)`: zero or more `item`s separated by the
//   spelling `sep`, with a trailing separator allowed when `trailing`. Always
//   matches (an empty list is a list); a separator with no item after it is
//   consumed when `trailing`, and left for the enclosing rule when not.
// - `Climb(atom, precs)`: precedence climbing — an `atom`, then binary operators
//   drawn from `precs`, nested by binding power. Each application nests under the
//   *enclosing* production's index, so `1 + 2 * 3` yields a node per operand
//   rather than one flat run.
variant Rule<K: variant> =
    | Term(K)
    | Spelling(str)
    | Then(Rule<K>[])
    | OneOf(Rule<K>[])
    | Many(Rule<K>, u64)
    | Maybe(Rule<K>)
    | Named(str)
    | NamedIdx(u64)
    | ListOf(Rule<K>, str, bool)
    | Climb(Rule<K>, Prec[])

// How a production's matched node becomes a value of the caller's AST type `A`:
// a function from the source text, the node itself, and the already-lowered
// values of its interior children (`nodes`, in match order). It may fail —
// a literal that overflows, a duplicate key — with a `ParseError` the driver
// passes straight through.
//
// A one-arm variant rather than a bare function type, so a later arm can be
// added without every grammar changing shape. It stays one arm deliberately: a
// payload-free `NoLower` would pin no `A` of its own, leaving nothing to infer
// the type parameter from, so a production with nothing to lower passes a
// trivial named function instead.
//
// Function values are non-capturing and non-generic (`check.rs`), so each is a
// named top-level `fn lower_x(src: str, node: Cst, kids: A[]) -> A!ParseError`.
// They also can't be compared with `==`, which is why the tests below assert on
// what a grammar *parses*, never on a grammar value — the same constraint
// `lexer.aipl` lives with.
variant Build<A: any> = Mk((str, Cst, A[]) -> A!ParseError)

// One named production: its rule, and what each downstream pass needs from it.
//
// `name` is both the reference target (`Named("expr")`) and the CST node's name.
// `expect_as` is the label an error blames when this production fails having
// matched nothing — the reified form of the LR parser's `SYMBOL_DISPLAY_NAMES`,
// which collapses `expr`/`term`/`unary`/`atom` to one word. Empty means
// *transparent*: a plumbing production never surfaces in a message, and the
// finer expectations from inside it are what the reader sees. `scope` is the
// highlighter hint; it is carried, not yet consumed.
struct Production<K: variant, A: any> {
    name: str,
    rule: Rule<K>,
    build: Build<A>,
    expect_as: str = "",
    scope: str = "",
}

// A whole language: its productions, in order. Position is identity — a
// `NamedIdx` and a `CTree` both name a production by its index here — so the
// order of this array is part of a linked grammar's meaning.
struct Grammar<K: variant, A: any> { prods: Production<K, A>[] }

// `r` repeated zero or more times (PEG `*`).
pub fn many<K: variant>(r: Rule<K>) -> Rule<K> { Many(r, 0) }

// `r` repeated one or more times (PEG `+`).
pub fn many1<K: variant>(r: Rule<K>) -> Rule<K> { Many(r, 1) }

// The production names of `g`, in index order — the table `show` renders a
// tree against, and what `link` resolves references through.
pub fn prod_names<K: variant, A: any>(g: Grammar<K, A>) -> str[] { g.prods.map(|p| p.name) }

// The index of `n` in `names`, or `none`. Names are few and lookups happen once
// per reference at link time, so a scan is the whole implementation.
pub fn name_index(names: str[], n: str) -> u64? {
    for (let i, x : names) {
        if (x == n) {
            return some(i);
        };
    }
    none
}

// One rule with every `Named(n)` in it rewritten to `NamedIdx(i)`. Fails when a
// reference names no production — the error a grammar author most wants caught
// before a parse rather than during one.
fn link_rule<K: variant>(r: Rule<K>, names: str[]) -> Rule<K>!ParseError {
    match (r) {
        Named(n) => match (name_index(names, n)) {
            some(i) => ok(NamedIdx(i)),
            none => err(ParseError { message: `no rule named "{n}"`, span: 0..0 }),
        },
        Then(rs) => ok(Then(link_rules(rs, names)?)),
        OneOf(rs) => ok(OneOf(link_rules(rs, names)?)),
        Many(sub, min) => ok(Many(link_rule(sub, names)?, min)),
        Maybe(sub) => ok(Maybe(link_rule(sub, names)?)),
        ListOf(item, sep, trailing) => ok(ListOf(link_rule(item, names)?, sep, trailing)),
        Climb(atom, precs) => ok(Climb(link_rule(atom, names)?, precs)),
        Term(k) => ok(Term(k)),
        Spelling(s) => ok(Spelling(s)),
        NamedIdx(i) => ok(NamedIdx(i)),
    }
}

fn link_rules<K: variant>(rs: Rule<K>[], names: str[]) -> Rule<K>[]!ParseError {
    rs.try_map(|r| link_rule(r, names))
}

pub fn link<K: variant, A: any>(g: Grammar<K, A>) -> Grammar<K, A>!ParseError {
    let names = prod_names(g);

    // Two productions of one name would make `Named` mean whichever came first
    // and leave the other unreachable — a typo that reads as a working grammar.
    for (let i, n : names) {
        match (name_index(names, n)) {
            some(j) => if (j != i) {
                return err(ParseError { message: `duplicate rule name "{n}"`, span: 0..0 });
            },
            none => {},
        };
    }
    let prods = g.prods.try_map(|p| ok(Production { ..p, rule: link_rule(p.rule, names)? }))?;
    ok(Grammar { prods })
}.doc("""
    `g` with every rule reference resolved: each `Named(name)` rewritten to the
    `NamedIdx(index)` of the production it names, so the driver resolves a
    reference by indexing rather than by comparing strings. Fails when a
    reference names no production, or when two productions share a name.

    Idempotent — a `NamedIdx` passes through — so a grammar may be linked once
    and reused, or handed to `parse` unlinked and linked per call.
""").test({
    // A tiny grammar over one token kind: `list -> "(" item* ")"`, `item -> atom
    // | list`. Enough to have a reference reach back through another production,
    // which is what linking has to get right.
    let r_list: Rule<Kind> = Then([Spelling("("), many(Named("item")), Spelling(")")]);
    let r_item: Rule<Kind> = OneOf([Term(Atom("")), Named("list")]);
    let g: Grammar<Kind, Node> = Grammar {
        prods: [
            Production { name: "list", rule: r_list, build: Mk(keep) },
            Production { name: "item", rule: r_item, build: Mk(keep), expect_as: "an item" },
        ],
    };

    assert(prod_names(g) == ["list", "item"]);
    assert(name_index(prod_names(g), "item") == some(1));
    assert(name_index(prod_names(g), "nope") == none);

    let linked = link(g)?;
    // `item`'s two alternatives: a terminal, and a reference now an index.
    assert(rule_of(linked, 1) == OneOf([Term(Atom("")), NamedIdx(0)]));

    // The reference nested two levels down inside `list` is rewritten too, and
    // everything around it is left alone.
    assert(rule_of(linked, 0) == Then([Spelling("("), Many(NamedIdx(1), 0), Spelling(")")]));
    // Linking preserves the fields a rewrite doesn't touch.
    assert(prod_names(linked) == ["list", "item"]);
    assert(expect_of(linked, 1) == "an item");

    // Idempotent: a linked grammar links again to itself.
    assert(rule_of(link(linked)?, 1) == OneOf([Term(Atom("")), NamedIdx(0)]));

    // Every rule shape carries its reference, and every one is rewritten.
    let shapes: Grammar<Kind, Node> = Grammar {
        prods: [
            Production { name: "a", rule: Maybe(Named("a")), build: Mk(keep) },
            Production { name: "b", rule: ListOf(Named("a"), ",", true), build: Mk(keep) },
            Production {
                name: "c",
                rule: Climb(Named("a"), [Prec { power: 1, right: false, ops: ["+"] }]),
                build: Mk(keep),
            },
            Production { name: "d", rule: many1(Named("b")), build: Mk(keep) },
        ],
    };
    let ls = link(shapes)?;
    assert(rule_of(ls, 0) == Maybe(NamedIdx(0)));
    assert(rule_of(ls, 1) == ListOf(NamedIdx(0), ",", true));
    assert(rule_of(ls, 2) == Climb(NamedIdx(0), [Prec { power: 1, right: false, ops: ["+"] }]));
    assert(rule_of(ls, 3) == Many(NamedIdx(1), 1));

    // An unresolved reference is a grammar error, named in the message.
    let bad: Grammar<Kind, Node> = Grammar {
        prods: [Production { name: "a", rule: Named("missing"), build: Mk(keep) }],
    };
    assert(link(bad) == err(ParseError { message: "no rule named \"missing\"", span: 0..0 }));

    // So is a duplicate name — the typo that otherwise reads as a working grammar.
    let dup: Grammar<Kind, Node> = Grammar {
        prods: [
            Production { name: "a", rule: Spelling("x"), build: Mk(keep) },
            Production { name: "a", rule: Spelling("y"), build: Mk(keep) },
        ],
    };
    assert(link(dup) == err(ParseError { message: "duplicate rule name \"a\"", span: 0..0 }));
})

// ---------- test-only fixtures ----------
//
// A token-kind type and an AST type for the `.test` block above. A real consumer
// declares its own pair; these exist because a `Grammar<K, A>` cannot be written
// down without both. (`Kind` rather than a `Kind` *constructor*: a constructor
// colliding with another top-level name in the same file is silently dropped —
// see the naming note in `PARSER_LIBRARY.md`.)
variant Kind = Atom(str) | Open | Close

variant Node = NAtom(str) | NList(Node[])

// The trivial lowering a production with nothing to lower passes, and all these
// fixtures need: `Build` has no payload-free arm to mean "don't".
fn keep(src: str, node: Cst, kids: Node[]) -> Node!ParseError { ok(NList(kids)) }

// The rule of production `i` of `g`, for the assertions above — a grammar value
// can't be compared with `==` (it holds function values), but the rule inside one
// can.
fn rule_of<K: variant, A: any>(g: Grammar<K, A>, i: u64) -> Rule<K> {
    match (g.prods[i]) {
        some(p) => p.rule,
        none => Spelling(""),
    }
}

fn expect_of<K: variant, A: any>(g: Grammar<K, A>, i: u64) -> str {
    match (g.prods[i]) {
        some(p) => p.expect_as,
        none => "",
    }
}
